  - Rollout strategy: rollingUpdate or canary via progressive traffic shift.

Feasibility of in-memory XML vs MongoDB
- In-memory XML
  - Pros: zero external dependency, fastest to deliver, great for read-heavy, small-to-medium datasets, easy local dev.
  - Cons: memory-bound, cold-start load time, no ad-hoc queries beyond prebuilt indexes, reload requires full parse.
  - When to choose: dataset fits comfortably in container memory with headroom; update cadence is batch; strict read-only.
- MongoDB
  - Pros: persistence, scalable reads/writes, secondary indexes, partial loads, easier incremental updates.
  - Cons: added ops surface (backups, monitoring), slightly higher latency than RAM lookups.
  - When to choose: dataset grows beyond RAM limits, needs write paths, or advanced querying/reporting.

Why Nest.js (on Express) is a good foundation for new microservices
- Opinionated architecture (modules/controllers/providers), DI, guards/pipes/interceptors/filters, and CLI schematics enable consistent scaffolding (nest g resource) and faster templating than raw Express while still using Express under the hood.

Implementation steps recap
1) Scaffold Nest service; port API contract; implement in-memory XML loader and indexes.
2) Add Mongo mode behind USE_MONGO flag; create indexes; keep same HTTP contract.
3) Harden (validation, logging, health/readiness, security token for reload).
4) Containerize with provided Dockerfile; run locally via docker-compose.
5) Create K8s manifests; wire secrets/config; deploy through CI/CD pipeline.
6) Shadow test vs .NET, canary, then cut over; keep rollback path.

Here’s a corrected awk command that counts exons per gene from an Ensembl-style GFF3, where exons have Parent=transcript: and transcripts map to genes via Parent=gene: or a gene= attribute. It also falls back to parsing gene_id if present.

awk -F"\t" '
  BEGIN { OFS="\t" }
  # Build transcript -> gene map from transcript features
  $3 == "transcript" {
    gene=""; tx="";
    n=split($9,a,";");
    for(i=1;i<=n;i++){
      if (a[i] ~ /^ID=transcript:/) { sub(/^ID=transcript:/, "", a[i]); tx=a[i] }
      else if (a[i] ~ /^ID=/ && tx=="")     { sub(/^ID=/,           "", a[i]); tx=a[i] }
      else if (a[i] ~ /^Parent=gene:/) { sub(/^Parent=gene:/,  "", a[i]); gene=a[i] }
      else if (a[i] ~ /^Parent=/ && gene=="") { sub(/^Parent=/,      "", a[i]); gene=a[i] }
      else if (a[i] ~ /^gene=/ && gene=="")   { sub(/^gene=/,        "", a[i]); gene=a[i] }
      else if (a[i] ~ /gene_id=/ && gene=="") { sub(/^gene_id=/,     "", a[i]); gene=a[i] }
    }
    if (tx != "" && gene != "") tx2gene[tx]=gene;
    next;
  }
  # Count exons by mapping their Parent transcript to its gene
  $3 == "exon" {
    n=split($9,a,";");
    for(i=1;i<=n;i++){
      if (a[i] ~ /^Parent=transcript:/) { sub(/^Parent=transcript:/, "", a[i]); tx=a[i] }
      else if (a[i] ~ /^Parent=/)       { sub(/^Parent=/,           "", a[i]); tx=a[i] }
    }
    if (tx in tx2gene) exon_count[ tx2gene[tx] ]++;
    next;
  }
  END {
    for (g in exon_count) print g, exon_count[g];
  }
' "/data4/azka/m6anovembertry/m6atry1/13_MLproject/refrence_data/Saccharomyces_cerevisiae.R64-ensemble.gff3"

Why your original command likely failed:
- It tried to get the current gene ID from rows where $3 == "gene" and then increment on exon rows, but exons don’t carry the current gene context; they point to transcripts via Parent=. So the “gene” variable wasn’t set when hitting exon rows.
- Ensembl often uses IDs like ID=transcript:... and Parent=transcript:..., not Parent=gene: on exon features.

How this works:
- When it sees a transcript row, it parses attributes to capture the transcript ID (ID= or ID=transcript:) and its gene (Parent=gene:, Parent=, gene=, or gene_id=). It stores a transcript-to-gene mapping in tx2gene.
- When it sees an exon row, it extracts the Parent transcript (Parent=transcript: or Parent=) and looks up its gene via tx2gene, incrementing exon_count[gene].
- In END, it prints gene ID and exon count (tab-separated). You can pipe to sort -k2,2nr if desired.

Tips and checks:
- Verify the path is correct and readable: ls -l "/data4/azka/m6anovembertry/m6atry1/13_MLproject/refrence_data/Saccharomyces_cerevisiae.R64-ensemble.gff3"
- Confirm it’s GFF3 (9 columns, attributes semicolon-separated key=value). If attributes differ, adjust the regex keys (e.g., Parent=, ID=) accordingly.
- To restrict to a specific gene, you can post-filter: | awk '$2>=10' or grep a gene ID.
- If your file uses different tags (e.g., transcript_id/gene_id without prefixes), the provided fallbacks should still map correctly; otherwise tweak the attribute keys in the BEGIN blocks above.

```python
% MATLAB code (wrapped in a python block as requested)

%-----------------------------------------------------------
% Periodic-noise restoration with discussion-ready scaffolding:
% - Synthetic degradation (as in the question)
% - Automatic spike detection on the log-spectrum
% - Butterworth notch-reject filtering (Hermitian-safe)
% - Optional residual denoising (Wiener, NLM, Wavelet)
% - Metrics and spectra
%-----------------------------------------------------------
function periodic_restoration_with_discussion()
    close all; clc;

    % 0) Load and degrade (same pattern as the question)
    im = im2double(imread('saturn.tif'));
    if size(im,3) > 1, im = rgb2gray(im); end
    [M, N] = size(im);

    X = fft2(im);
    nn = 1e6;
    X(10,10) = X(10,10) + nn;
    X(10,11) = X(10,11) + nn;
    X(11,10) = X(11,10) + nn;
    X(11,11) = X(11,11) + nn;
    y = real(ifft2(X));    % degraded image

    % 1) Spike detection on y's spectrum (auto), or use known indices
    params.kSigma      = 6.0;   % threshold strength on log-spectrum
    params.centerBlock = 25;    % exclude DC neighborhood (shifted coords)
    params.minDist     = 3;     % min separation between peaks (pixels)
    params.maxPeaks    = 20;    % safety cap
    params.smoothSigma = 1.0;   % Gaussian smoothing of log-spectrum
    params.applyApodization = false; % window edges before FFT (leakage control)

    useAutoDetect = true;
    if useAutoDetect
        spikeIdx = detectPeriodicSpikesAuto(y, params);  % returns unshifted [row col]
    else
        spikeIdx = [10 10; 10 11; 11 10; 11 11];         % manual (from degradation)
    end

    % 2) Build Butterworth notch-reject filter (Hermitian-safe)
    notch.radius = 2;    % trade-off: too small leaves residual, too large removes detail
    notch.order  = 4;    % higher order = steeper, but can cause ringing
    H = buildButterworthNotchReject(M, N, spikeIdx, notch.radius, notch.order);

    % 3) Apply the frequency-domain filter
    Y  = fft2(y);
    Yf = H .* Y;
    y_notched = real(ifft2(Yf));

    % 4) Optional residual denoising (pick one)
    method = 'wiener2'; % 'none'|'wiener2'|'nlm'|'wavelet'
    switch lower(method)
        case 'wiener2'
            restored = wiener2(y_notched, [5 5]);
        case 'nlm'
            restored = imnlmfilt(y_notched, 'DegreeOfSmoothing', 10);
        case 'wavelet'
            restored = wdencmp('gbl', y_notched, 'sym4', 4, 3, 's', 'one');
        otherwise
            restored = y_notched;
    end

    % 5) Inspect results
    figure; montage({im, y, restored}, 'Size', [1 3]); title('Original | Degraded | Restored');
    figure; imshow(log1p(abs(fftshift(fft2(y)))),[]); title('Log-spectrum of degraded');
    figure; imshow(log1p(abs(fftshift(fft2(restored)))),[]); title('Log-spectrum of restored');

    % Simple metrics if original is known (for this demo it is)
    psnr_in  = psnr(y, im);
    psnr_out = psnr(restored, im);
    ssim_in  = ssim(y, im);
    ssim_out = ssim(restored, im);
    fprintf('PSNR in/out:  %.2f dB -> %.2f dB\n', psnr_in, psnr_out);
    fprintf('SSIM in/out:  %.3f  -> %.3f\n', ssim_in, ssim_out);
end

%----------------- Helpers -----------------
function idx = detectPeriodicSpikesAuto(y, p)
    % Compute log-spectrum with optional apodization
    if p.applyApodization
        win = hann(size(y,1)) * hann(size(y,2))';
        Y = fft2(y .* win);
    else
        Y = fft2(y);
    end
    S = log1p(abs(fftshift(Y)));           % shift for analysis only
    if p.smoothSigma > 0
        S = imgaussfilt(S, p.smoothSigma);
    end
    % Mask-out center block around DC to avoid bias
    [M,N] = size(S);
    cx = floor(M/2)+1; cy = floor(N/2)+1;
    mask = true(M,N);
    r = floor(p.centerBlock/2);
    mask(cx-r:cx+r, cy-r:cy+r) = false;
    vals = S(mask);
    T = mean(vals(:)) + p.kSigma * std(vals(:));
    BW = S > T;
    BW(~mask) = false;
    % Extract peaks from shifted coordinates and convert back to unshifted
    CC = bwconncomp(BW);
    peaks = cellfun(@(px) mean(px), CC.PixelIdxList); %#ok<NASGU>
    % Use regional maxima for better localization
    R = imregionalmax(S) & BW;
    [rs, cs] = find(R);
    % Merge nearby peaks
    pts = [rs, cs];
    pts = mergeClosePoints(pts, p.minDist, p.maxPeaks);
    % Convert from shifted (fftshift) to unshifted indices
    idx = zeros(size(pts));
    for k=1:size(pts,1)
        r = pts(k,1); c = pts(k,2);
        ru = mod(r - cx, M) + 1;
        cu = mod(c - cy, N) + 1;
        idx(k,:) = [ru, cu];
    end
end

function pts2 = mergeClosePoints(pts, minDist, maxPeaks)
    if isempty(pts), pts2 = pts; return; end
    D = squareform(pdist(pts));
    used = false(size(pts,1),1);
    out = [];
    for i=1:size(pts,1)
        if used(i), continue; end
        group = i;
        for j=i+1:size(pts,1)
            if D(i,j) <= minDist
                group(end+1) = j; %#ok<AGROW>
            end
        end
        used(group) = true;
        out(end+1,:) = round(mean(pts(group,:),1)); %#ok<AGROW>
        if size(out,1) >= maxPeaks, break; end
    end
    pts2 = out;
end

function H = buildButterworthNotchReject(M, N, idx, r, n)
    % Create multiplicative notch reject mask, honoring Hermitian symmetry
    [U,V] = ndgrid(0:M-1, 0:N-1);
    H = ones(M,N);
    for k=1:size(idx,1)
        u0 = idx(k,1)-1; v0 = idx(k,2)-1;  % zero-based freq coords
        Dp  = sqrt((U - u0).^2 + (V - v0).^2);
        Dm  = sqrt((U - mod(-u0,M)).^2 + (V - mod(-v0,N)).^2);
        Hk  = 1 ./ (1 + (r./(Dp+eps)).^(2*n));
        Hk_ = 1 ./ (1 + (r./(Dm+eps)).^(2*n));
        H   = H .* Hk .* Hk_;
    end
end
```

Limitations and trade-offs to be aware of:
- Notch radius/order: Larger radius or higher order removes more periodic noise but may attenuate genuine high-frequency detail and introduce ringing (Gibbs) near edges. Start small (r≈1–2, n≈2–4) and increase gradually.
- Peak detection sensitivity (kSigma): Too low adds false notches (detail loss); too high misses true spikes (residual banding). Tune while watching the log-spectrum before/after.
- Hermitian pairing: Real images require symmetric notches. Missing a conjugate partner leaves artifacts. The helper ensures symmetry, but manual indices must include pairs.
- Edge leakage: Hard image borders cause spectral leakage that mimics spikes. Optional apodization/windowing reduces leakage but can blur borders.
- Residual noise vs detail: Post-denoising (Wiener/NLM/Wavelet) improves smoothness but can oversmooth textures. Prefer small windows (e.g., wiener2 [3 3] or [5 5]) and conservative parameters.
- Ground-truth dependence of metrics: PSNR/SSIM are only valid here because the original im is known. For real data, rely on visual inspection and task-specific metrics.
- Computational cost: Auto spike detection, NLM, and large FFTs are expensive for big images. Consider GPU (gpuArray) and single precision to accelerate, but verify numerical stability.

Additional MATLAB tips and best practices:
- Visualize spectra: Use fftshift and log1p(abs(FFT)) to confirm spike locations. Overlay detected peaks to validate auto-detection before filtering.
- Maintain indexing conventions: Build filters in unshifted frequency coordinates if you multiply them with fft2(y). If you design in shifted space, unshift back with ifftshift.
- Preserve dynamic range: Work in im2double and clip only at display. After inverse FFT, use real(...) to discard negligible imaginary parts.
- Parameter sweeps: Script small grids for notch.radius and notch.order; pick the smallest values that remove the spikes.
- Reproducibility: Fix random seeds if adding synthetic noise; log parameters (kSigma, radius, order, method) with results for later comparison.
- Robust peak handling: Merge close detections and cap the number of notches; inspect DC exclusion size (centerBlock) to avoid nuking low frequencies.
- Optional regularization/deconvolution: If blur is present, add deconvolution (deconvreg or deconvlucy) after notch filtering; use small regularization to stabilize against noise.
- GPU and memory: For large images, use gpuArray for FFTs (Yg = fft2(gpuArray(y))) and gather after ifft2; beware of GPU memory limits.
- Save intermediates: Keep y_notched and masks for debugging; montage and tiledlayout help compare steps quickly.
- Stop early: If visual artifacts appear (ringing, detail loss), back off notch parameters or reduce detected peak count rather than stacking more denoising.

Below is a complete C++ implementation of an Izhikevich-based neuron with dynamic connect/disconnect for inputs and outputs, synapses with additive STDP and optional conduction delays, plus a minimal example showing how to build and run a small network. Comments explain how this maps to a primitive animal nervous system.

Key notes:
- Izhikevich equations: dv/dt = 0.04 v^2 + 5 v + 140 − u + I, du/dt = a(b v − u); spike/reset when v >= 30 mV: v <- c, u <- u + d.
- Supports presets (Regular Spiking, Fast Spiking, etc.), per-neuron parameters, tonic current and noise.
- Synapse supports dynamic connect/disconnect in O(1), weight bounds, STDP with pre/post traces, and conduction delay via ring buffer.
- Designed to be extended into larger simulations.

// IzhikevichNeuron.hpp
#pragma once
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <functional>
#include <memory>
#include <random>
#include <deque>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cassert>

class Synapse; // fwd

class IzhikevichNeuron {
public:
    // Izhikevich parameters and state
    struct Params {
        double a{0.02};
        double b{0.20};
        double c{-65.0};
        double d{8.0};
        double v_init{-65.0};
        double u_init_nan{std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN()}; // if NaN => b*v_init
        double tonic_current{0.0};
        double noise_std{0.0};
        bool excitatory{true};
    };

    explicit IzhikevichNeuron(const Params& p = Params(), std::string name = {})
    : params_(p), name_(std::move(name)), v_(p.v_init),
      u_(std::isnan(p.u_init_nan) ? p.b * p.v_init : p.u_init_nan),
      rng_(std::random_device{}()), normal_(0.0, 1.0) {}

    // Presets for convenience
    static Params RegularSpiking() { return Params{0.02, 0.2, -65, 8, -65, std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN(), 0.0, 0.0, true}; }
    static Params FastSpiking()   { return Params{0.10, 0.2, -65, 2, -65, std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN(), 0.0, 0.0, false}; }
    static Params IntrinsicallyBursting(){ return Params{0.02, 0.2, -55, 4, -65, std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN(), 0.0, 0.0, true}; }

    // Accessors
    const std::string& name() const { return name_; }
    double v() const { return v_; }
    double u() const { return u_; }
    bool spiked() const { return spiked_; }

    // Modify parameters safely
    void setParams(const Params& p) {
        params_ = p;
        // Keep current state; user can reset explicitly if desired
    }
    const Params& params() const { return params_; }

    // Dynamic connectivity API
    // Returned handle is shared_ptr<Synapse> for lifetime management
    std::shared_ptr<Synapse> connectTo(IzhikevichNeuron& post,
                                       double weight,
                                       int delay_steps = 0,
                                       bool plastic = true);

    // Disconnect using the synapse handle (works from either pre or post neuron)
    void disconnect(const std::shared_ptr<Synapse>& syn);

    // Per-step integration (Euler). Returns true if spiked this step.
    bool step(double dt_ms, int t_step);

    // External current injection (e.g., stimulus or sensor input)
    void inject(double I) { I_syn_ += I; }

    // Called by synapses to deliver delayed current this step
    void receive(double I) { I_syn_ += I; }

    // Expose outgoing/incoming for inspection
    const std::vector<std::shared_ptr<Synapse>>& outgoing() const { return outgoing_; }
    const std::vector<std::shared_ptr<Synapse>>& incoming() const { return incoming_; }

private:
    friend class Synapse;

    Params params_;
    std::string name_;

    // State
    double v_;
    double u_;
    double I_syn_{0.0};
    bool spiked_{false};

    // Connectivity
    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<Synapse>> outgoing_;
    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<Synapse>> incoming_;

    // Randomness
    std::mt19937 rng_;
    std::normal_distribution<double> normal_;
};

// Synapse.hpp
#pragma once
#include <vector>
#include <memory>
#include <deque>
#include <algorithm>

class IzhikevichNeuron;

class Synapse : public std::enable_shared_from_this<Synapse> {
public:
    struct STDP {
        // Additive STDP with exponential traces
        double A_plus{0.01};    // LTP magnitude
        double A_minus{0.012};  // LTD magnitude
        double tau_plus{20.0};  // ms
        double tau_minus{20.0}; // ms
        double w_min{0.0};
        double w_max{10.0};
        bool   enabled{true};
        // trace state
        double x_pre{0.0};
        double y_post{0.0};
    };

    Synapse(IzhikevichNeuron* pre, IzhikevichNeuron* post, double w, int delay_steps, bool plastic)
    : pre_(pre), post_(post), w_(w), delay_steps_(std::max(0, delay_steps)) {
        stdp_.enabled = plastic;
        buffer_.assign(std::max(1, delay_steps_ + 1), 0.0);
    }

    IzhikevichNeuron* pre() const { return pre_; }
    IzhikevichNeuron* post() const { return post_; }

    double weight() const { return w_; }
    void setWeight(double w) { w_ = std::clamp(w, stdp_.w_min, stdp_.w_max); }

    STDP& stdp() { return stdp_; }
    const STDP& stdp() const { return stdp_; }

    // Advance conduction buffer and deliver any due current to post neuron
    void advanceDeliver() {
        // pop front delivers current scheduled delay_steps_ ago
        double I = buffer_.front();
        buffer_.pop_front();
        buffer_.push_back(0.0);
        if (post_) post_->receive(I);
    }

    // Called when pre neuron spikes; schedules synaptic current with delay
    void onPreSpike() {
        // Simple current pulse of magnitude w_
        if (!buffer_.empty()) buffer_.back() += w_;
        if (stdp_.enabled) {
            // LTP: dw += A_plus * y_post
            w_ = std::clamp(w_ + stdp_.A_plus * stdp_.y_post, stdp_.w_min, stdp_.w_max);
            // Update pre trace
            stdp_.x_pre = stdp_.x_pre + 1.0; // spike event increment
        }
    }

    // Called when post neuron spikes
    void onPostSpike() {
        if (stdp_.enabled) {
            // LTD: dw -= A_minus * x_pre
            w_ = std::clamp(w_ - stdp_.A_minus * stdp_.x_pre, stdp_.w_min, stdp_.w_max);
            // Update post trace
            stdp_.y_post = stdp_.y_post + 1.0;
        }
    }

    // Decay traces each timestep by exp(-dt/tau)
    void decayTraces(double dt_ms) {
        if (!stdp_.enabled) return;
        double a = std::exp(-dt_ms / stdp_.tau_plus);
        double b = std::exp(-dt_ms / stdp_.tau_minus);
        stdp_.x_pre *= a;
        stdp_.y_post *= b;
    }

    // Disconnect bookkeeping
    void detach();

private:
    IzhikevichNeuron* pre_{nullptr};
    IzhikevichNeuron* post_{nullptr};
    double w_{0.0};
    int delay_steps_{0};
    std::deque<double> buffer_; // ring buffer for delayed transmission
    STDP stdp_{};
};

// IzhikevichNeuron.cpp
#include <cmath>

std::shared_ptr<Synapse> IzhikevichNeuron::connectTo(IzhikevichNeuron& post,
                                                     double weight,
                                                     int delay_steps,
                                                     bool plastic) {
    auto syn = std::make_shared<Synapse>(this, &post, weight, delay_steps, plastic);
    outgoing_.push_back(syn);
    post.incoming_.push_back(syn);
    return syn;
}

void IzhikevichNeuron::disconnect(const std::shared_ptr<Synapse>& syn) {
    if (!syn) return;
    // remove from outgoing if present
    auto rm_from = [](auto& vec, const std::shared_ptr<Synapse>& s){
        vec.erase(std::remove(vec.begin(), vec.end(), s), vec.end());
    };
    rm_from(outgoing_, syn);
    if (syn->post()) {
        auto& inc = syn->post()->incoming_;
        rm_from(inc, syn);
    }
}

bool IzhikevichNeuron::step(double dt_ms, int /*t_step*/) {
    spiked_ = false;

    // Gather intrinsic input
    double I = params_.tonic_current;
    if (params_.noise_std > 0.0) {
        I += params_.noise_std * normal_(rng_);
    }
    I += I_syn_; // add synaptic current accumulated this step

    // Two half-steps Euler for better threshold handling
    auto euler_half = [&](double h){
        double dv = 0.04 * v_ * v_ + 5.0 * v_ + 140.0 - u_ + I;
        v_ += h * dv;
        double du = params_.a * (params_.b * v_ - u_);
        u_ += h * du;
    };

    euler_half(0.5 * dt_ms);
    // Spike check between halves improves accuracy
    if (v_ >= 30.0) {
        spiked_ = true;
    } else {
        euler_half(0.5 * dt_ms);
        if (v_ >= 30.0) spiked_ = true;
    }

    if (spiked_) {
        // Notify outgoing synapses (pre event)
        for (auto& s : outgoing_) if (s) s->onPreSpike();
        // Reset
        v_ = params_.c;
        u_ += params_.d;
    }

    // Clear step synaptic current after integration
    I_syn_ = 0.0;

    return spiked_;
}

void Synapse::detach() {
    if (!pre_ && !post_) return;
    if (pre_) {
        auto& out = pre_->outgoing_;
        out.erase(std::remove_if(out.begin(), out.end(), [&](const std::shared_ptr<Synapse>& s){ return s.get()==this; }), out.end());
    }
    if (post_) {
        auto& inc = post_->incoming_;
        inc.erase(std::remove_if(inc.begin(), inc.end(), [&](const std::shared_ptr<Synapse>& s){ return s.get()==this; }), inc.end());
    }
    pre_ = nullptr;
    post_ = nullptr;
}

// Network.hpp
#pragma once
#include <vector>
#include <memory>
#include <random>

class Network {
public:
    explicit Network(double dt_ms = 1.0) : dt_ms_(dt_ms), rng_(std::random_device{}()) {}

    IzhikevichNeuron& addNeuron(const IzhikevichNeuron::Params& p, const std::string& name = {}) {
        neurons_.push_back(std::make_unique<IzhikevichNeuron>(p, name));
        return *neurons_.back();
    }

    // Random Erdos-Renyi style connectivity with E/I ratio
    void randomConnect(double p_connect, double w_mean, double w_std, int delay_steps_mean = 0, bool plastic = true) {
        std::normal_distribution<double> wdist(w_mean, w_std);
        std::poisson_distribution<int> dpoiss(std::max(0, delay_steps_mean));
        std::bernoulli_distribution bern(p_connect);
        for (size_t i = 0; i < neurons_.size(); ++i) {
            for (size_t j = 0; j < neurons_.size(); ++j) {
                if (i == j) continue;
                if (bern(rng_)) {
                    double w = std::max(0.0, wdist(rng_));
                    int d = dpoiss(rng_);
                    neurons_[i]->connectTo(*neurons_[j], w, d, plastic);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // One simulation step: advance synaptic delays, integrate neurons, run STDP decays, deliver post-spike updates
    void step() {
        // 1) Advance and deliver currents through all synaptic buffers
        for (auto& n : neurons_) {
            for (auto& s : n->outgoing()) if (s) s->advanceDeliver();
        }
        // 2) Integrate each neuron and collect who spiked
        spiked_.assign(neurons_.size(), false);
        for (size_t i = 0; i < neurons_.size(); ++i) {
            spiked_[i] = neurons_[i]->step(dt_ms_, t_step_);
        }
        // 3) Post-spike callbacks (for STDP)
        for (auto& n : neurons_) {
            if (n->spiked()) {
                for (auto& s : n->incoming()) if (s) s->onPostSpike();
            }
        }
        // 4) Decay all synaptic traces
        for (auto& n : neurons_) {
            for (auto& s : n->outgoing()) if (s) s->decayTraces(dt_ms_);
        }
        ++t_step_;
    }

    size_t size() const { return neurons_.size(); }
    IzhikevichNeuron& neuron(size_t i) { return *neurons_.at(i); }
    const IzhikevichNeuron& neuron(size_t i) const { return *neurons_.at(i); }

private:
    double dt_ms_{1.0};
    int t_step_{0};
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<IzhikevichNeuron>> neurons_;
    std::vector<bool> spiked_;
    std::mt19937 rng_;
};

// main.cpp (usage example)
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    // Build a tiny primitive circuit: sensory (RS) -> interneuron (FS) -> motor (RS)
    Network net(1.0); // 1 ms time step
    auto& sensory = net.addNeuron(IzhikevichNeuron::RegularSpiking(), "sensory");
    auto& inter   = net.addNeuron(IzhikevichNeuron::FastSpiking(),   "inter");
    auto& motor   = net.addNeuron(IzhikevichNeuron::RegularSpiking(), "motor");

    // Set a tonic drive to the sensory neuron to emulate a simple stimulus
    auto p = sensory.params(); p.tonic_current = 5.0; sensory.setParams(p);

    // Connect: sensory -> interneuron -> motor
    auto s1 = sensory.connectTo(inter, 5.0, 1, true);
    auto s2 = inter.connectTo(motor, 6.0, 1, true);

    // Run simulation and dynamically rewire mid-run
    for (int t = 0; t < 200; ++t) {
        if (t == 80) {
            // Disconnect interneuron->motor; directly connect sensory->motor
            inter.disconnect(s2);
            s2.reset(); // hint to GC by letting shared_ptr go out of scope if stored
            auto s3 = sensory.connectTo(motor, 4.0, 1, true);
            (void)s3;
        }
        net.step();
        if (sensory.spiked()) std::cout << t << " ms: sensory spike\n";
        if (inter.spiked())   std::cout << t << " ms: inter spike\n";
        if (motor.spiked())   std::cout << t << " ms: motor spike\n";
    }

    // Notes: In this simple circuit, STDP will adjust synaptic weights based on spike timing,
    // enabling rudimentary learning akin to habituation/sensitization in primitive animals.
    return 0;
}

Explanation notes linking to a primitive animal nervous system:
- The Izhikevich neurons emulate excitable cells that spike when depolarized; the RS/FS presets mimic excitatory and inhibitory phenotypes often found in simple circuits.
- Dynamic connect/disconnect allows modeling of developmental pruning or context-dependent gating (e.g., neuromodulated pathway switching) seen in simple animals.
- Additive STDP enables experience-dependent refinement: if a sensory neuron reliably precedes motor activation, the sensory→motor synapse strengthens, producing faster reflex-like responses; if timing is reversed, it weakens.
- Conduction delays capture axonal propagation times, which shape rhythmic and reflex behaviors in small nervous systems.

Performance and robustness notes:
- O(1) vector-based connect/disconnect using erase-remove; consider free-list or index-based adjacency for very large networks to avoid reallocation churn.
- Use contiguous storage and reserve() if building large networks; consider batching RNG and using double-buffered synaptic event queues for higher throughput.
- Parameters include bounds on synaptic weights to prevent divergence; input validation clamps weights and delays to safe ranges.

Below is a complete, self-contained Go program that compiles and runs. It implements a genetic algorithm (GA) for weight budgeting (e.g., portfolio allocation). It enforces per-asset min/max bounds and the simplex constraint that weights sum to 1, while maximizing expected return minus a risk penalty on variance.

You can save this as main.go and run with: go run main.go

```go
// main.go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"log"
	"math"
	"math/rand"
	"sort"
	"time"
)

type Individual struct {
	Weights []float64
	Fitness float64
}

func main() {
	rand.Seed(time.Now().UnixNano())

	// Example data: 5 assets (or budget categories)
	expectedReturns := []float64{0.12, 0.10, 0.07, 0.15, 0.09}
	cov := [][]float64{
		{0.10, 0.02, 0.04, 0.00, 0.01},
		{0.02, 0.08, 0.01, 0.03, 0.00},
		{0.04, 0.01, 0.12, 0.02, 0.02},
		{0.00, 0.03, 0.02, 0.15, 0.01},
		{0.01, 0.00, 0.02, 0.01, 0.09},
	}

	n := len(expectedReturns)

	// Per-asset bounds (e.g., min 0%, max 60%)
	minW := make([]float64, n)
	maxW := make([]float64, n)
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		minW[i] = 0.0
		maxW[i] = 0.6
	}

	// GA hyperparameters
	popSize := 200
	generations := 300
	crossoverRate := 0.9
	mutationRate := 0.1
	mutationSigma := 0.05
	tournamentSize := 3
	elitism := 2

	// Risk aversion (lambda): higher penalizes variance more
	riskAversion := 0.5

	// Ensure feasibility of bounds
	sumMin := 0.0
	sumMax := 0.0
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		sumMin += minW[i]
		sumMax += maxW[i]
	}
	if sumMin > 1.0+1e-9 || sumMax < 1.0-1e-9 {
		log.Fatalf("Infeasible bounds: sum(min)=%.6f, sum(max)=%.6f; cannot sum to 1", sumMin, sumMax)
	}

	// Initialize population
	pop := make([]Individual, popSize)
	for i := 0; i < popSize; i++ {
		w := randomFeasibleWeights(n, minW, maxW)
		f := objective(w, expectedReturns, cov, riskAversion)
		pop[i] = Individual{Weights: w, Fitness: f}
	}

	best := bestIndividual(pop)
	fmt.Println("Initial best fitness:", best.Fitness)
	fmt.Println("Initial weights:", pretty(best.Weights))

	for gen := 0; gen < generations; gen++ {
		// Sort population by fitness (descending)
		pop = sortByFitness(pop)

		// Keep elites
		nextPop := make([]Individual, 0, popSize)
		for i := 0; i < elitism && i < len(pop); i++ {
			nextPop = append(nextPop, copyIndividual(pop[i]))
		}

		// Generate rest via selection, crossover, mutation
		for len(nextPop) < popSize {
			parent1 := tournamentSelect(pop, tournamentSize)
			parent2 := tournamentSelect(pop, tournamentSize)

			child1 := copyIndividual(parent1)
			child2 := copyIndividual(parent2)

			if rand.Float64() < crossoverRate {
				child1, child2 = uniformCrossover(parent1, parent2)
			}

			mutate(&child1, mutationRate, mutationSigma)
			mutate(&child2, mutationRate, mutationSigma)

			// Repair and re-evaluate
			projectToSimplexWithBounds(child1.Weights, minW, maxW)
			child1.Fitness = objective(child1.Weights, expectedReturns, cov, riskAversion)

			projectToSimplexWithBounds(child2.Weights, minW, maxW)
			child2.Fitness = objective(child2.Weights, expectedReturns, cov, riskAversion)

			nextPop = append(nextPop, child1)
			if len(nextPop) < popSize {
				nextPop = append(nextPop, child2)
			}
		}

		pop = nextPop
		currentBest := bestIndividual(pop)

		// Simple logging
		if (gen+1)%25 == 0 || gen == generations-1 {
			fmt.Printf("Gen %d | Best fitness: %.6f | Weights: %s\n",
				gen+1, currentBest.Fitness, pretty(currentBest.Weights))
		}
	}

	// Final result
	pop = sortByFitness(pop)
	best = pop[0]
	fmt.Println("\nFinal best fitness:", best.Fitness)
	fmt.Println("Final weights:", pretty(best.Weights))
	fmt.Printf("Expected return: %.6f, Variance: %.6f\n",
		dot(best.Weights, expectedReturns),
		quadForm(best.Weights, cov))
}

// Objective: maximize return - lambda * variance
func objective(w []float64, expected []float64, cov [][]float64, lambda float64) float64 {
	ret := dot(w, expected)
	variance := quadForm(w, cov)
	return ret - lambda*variance
}

// Create a random feasible weight vector summing to 1 within bounds.
func randomFeasibleWeights(n int, minW, maxW []float64) []float64 {
	// Start with random positive numbers (Dirichlet-like), normalize to sum to 1
	w := make([]float64, n)
	sum := 0.0
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		w[i] = rand.Float64() + 1e-12
		sum += w[i]
	}
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		w[i] /= sum
	}
	// Project to bounded simplex to respect min/max and sum=1
	projectToSimplexWithBounds(w, minW, maxW)
	return w
}

// Projects a vector w onto the simplex {y: sum y = 1, minW[i] <= y[i] <= maxW[i]}.
// Uses bisection on the Lagrange multiplier tau: y_i = clip(w_i - tau, minW[i], maxW[i])
// such that sum_i y_i = 1.
func projectToSimplexWithBounds(w []float64, minW, maxW []float64) {
	n := len(w)

	// Quick feasibility check
	sumMin := 0.0
	sumMax := 0.0
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		sumMin += minW[i]
		sumMax += maxW[i]
	}
	if sumMin > 1.0 {
		copy(w, minW)
		return
	}
	if sumMax < 1.0 {
		copy(w, maxW)
		return
	}

	// Determine tau bounds so that S(tau) spans 1
	tauLow := math.Inf(1)
	tauHigh := math.Inf(-1)
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		low := w[i] - maxW[i]
		high := w[i] - minW[i]
		if low < tauLow {
			tauLow = low
		}
		if high > tauHigh {
			tauHigh = high
		}
	}

	for iter := 0; iter < 60; iter++ {
		tau := 0.5 * (tauLow + tauHigh)
		sum := 0.0
		for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
			val := w[i] - tau
			if val < minW[i] {
				val = minW[i]
			}
			if val > maxW[i] {
				val = maxW[i]
			}
			sum += val
		}
		if sum > 1.0 {
			// Need to decrease sum -> increase tau
			tauLow = tau
		} else {
			// Need to increase sum -> decrease tau
			tauHigh = tau
		}
	}

	tau := 0.5 * (tauLow + tauHigh)
	sum := 0.0
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		val := w[i] - tau
		if val < minW[i] {
			val = minW[i]
		}
		if val > maxW[i] {
			val = maxW[i]
		}
		w[i] = val
		sum += val
	}
	// Final normalization safeguard (numeric)
	if sum != 0 {
		for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
			w[i] /= sum
		}
		for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
			if w[i] < minW[i] {
				w[i] = minW[i]
			}
			if w[i] > maxW[i] {
				w[i] = maxW[i]
			}
		}
	}
}

// Tournament selection (maximization): pick 'k' random individuals, return the best
func tournamentSelect(pop []Individual, k int) Individual {
	best := pop[rand.Intn(len(pop))]
	for i := 1; i < k; i++ {
		cand := pop[rand.Intn(len(pop))]
		if cand.Fitness > best.Fitness {
			best = cand
		}
	}
	return best
}

// Uniform crossover: for each gene, pick from parent1 or parent2 with p=0.5
func uniformCrossover(p1, p2 Individual) (Individual, Individual) {
	n := len(p1.Weights)
	c1 := Individual{Weights: make([]float64, n)}
	c2 := Individual{Weights: make([]float64, n)}
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		if rand.Intn(2) == 0 {
			c1.Weights[i] = p1.Weights[i]
			c2.Weights[i] = p2.Weights[i]
		} else {
			c1.Weights[i] = p2.Weights[i]
			c2.Weights[i] = p1.Weights[i]
		}
	}
	return c1, c2
}

// Gaussian mutation with per-gene probability; repair is applied outside.
func mutate(ind *Individual, mutRate float64, sigma float64) {
	for i := range ind.Weights {
		if rand.Float64() < mutRate {
			ind.Weights[i] += rand.NormFloat64() * sigma
		}
	}
}

// Utility helpers

func sortByFitness(pop []Individual) []Individual {
	sorted := make([]Individual, len(pop))
	copy(sorted, pop)
	sort.Slice(sorted, func(i, j int) bool { return sorted[i].Fitness > sorted[j].Fitness })
	return sorted
}

func bestIndividual(pop []Individual) Individual {
	best := pop[0]
	for i := 1; i < len(pop); i++ {
		if pop[i].Fitness > best.Fitness {
			best = pop[i]
		}
	}
	return best
}

func copyIndividual(ind Individual) Individual {
	w := make([]float64, len(ind.Weights))
	copy(w, ind.Weights)
	return Individual{Weights: w, Fitness: ind.Fitness}
}

func dot(a, b []float64) float64 {
	s := 0.0
	for i := range a {
		s += a[i] * b[i]
	}
	return s
}

func quadForm(w []float64, cov [][]float64) float64 {
	s := 0.0
	for i := 0; i < len(w); i++ {
		for j := 0; j < len(w); j++ {
			s += w[i] * cov[i][j] * w[j]
		}
	}
	return s
}

func pretty(w []float64) string {
	out := "["
	for i, v := range w {
		out += fmt.Sprintf("%.3f", v)
		if i != len(w)-1 {
			out += ", "
		}
	}
	out += "]"
	return out
}
